# bau

A JavaScript DSL to define objects in a fluent way.

```
npm install bau
```


## Usage

```javascript
var bau = require('bau');
```


### .create()

Create a new instance to add modifiers.

```javascript
var b = bau.create();
```


### .define(name, fn)

Define a modifier `fn` on `.prototype` and make it chainable, i.e. it will return `this` in case `fn` returns nothing.

```javascript
bau.define('foo', function (name, delta) {
  this.push(function (obj) {
    obj[name] += delta;
  });
});

var b = bau.create().foo('bar', 1);

var obj = b.apply({ bar: 2 });
// => obj.bar === 3;
```


### .fork()

Create a separate DSL inheriting all existing extensions.
Modifications to the new DSL will not apply to its ancestor.

```javascript
var bau2 = bau.fork();
```


### .use(fn)

Calls the given function with `this` as first argument.
It is a shorthand for applying extensions.

```javascript
bau.use(function (bau) {
  // Define extensions here.
});
```


### .with

A shorthand for `.create()` to provide a more concise fluent DSL.

```javascript
bau.with.someExtension();
// vs
bau.create().someExtension();
```



### #apply(obj)

Apply all modifiers in the order they were added to a given object and return that object.


### #create(proto)

Call `#apply` on a new object created from the given prototype or `null` if none is given.


### #push(fn)

Add a modifier function to be applied to objects passed to `#apply`.

```javascript
b.push(function (obj) {
  obj.foo = 42;
});

var obj = b.apply({});
// => obj.foo === 42;
```

DSL instances may also be used as modifiers.
This has the same effect as using a function to call `#apply` on the DSL instanced to be used as modifier.

```javascript
var b2 = bau.with.push(function (obj) {
  obj.foo = 17;
});

b.push(b2);

var obj = b.apply({});
// => obj.foo === 17;
```

_Notice that the library does not detect eventual cycles (e.g. adding a DSL instance to its own sequence of modifiers)_.


### #scope(name[, fn])

Get a DSL to operate on some property `name`.
When given, function `fn` will be called with the scoped DSL.
In this variant `#scope` is chainable on the original DSL.
When `fn` is not given the scoped DSL will be returned.
In either case `.parent` can be used to access the parent DSL.

All modifiers will be appended to the top-most parent DSL and will be called with properly resolved objects according to the scope path.

```javascript
b.scope('foo', function (b) {
  b.push(function (obj) {
    obj.bar = 97;
  });
});

b.apply({});
// => exception because .foo cannot be resolved

var obj = b.apply({ foo: {} });
// => obj.foo.bar === 97;
```

The above usage of `#scope` is equivalent to the following non-callback form.

```javascript
var _b = b.scope('foo')
  .push(function (obj) {
    obj.bar = 97;
  });
// => _b.parent === b;
```


## Bundled Modifiers

Bundled modifiers are only applied when importing via `require('bau')` which will use a fork of `require('bau/lib/bau')`.


### #accessor(...names)

Define ES5 getters and setters for the given names which are either given as multiple arguments or a single array.
The getters and setters are defined as non-enumerable, non-configurable, and non-writable.
If you only want getters _or_ setters (but not both) use `#getter` or `#setter` respectively because getter and setter are defined via a single property which cannot be re-defined afterwards.
They simply delegate to regular methods whose name is determined by capitalizing a name's first character (`String#toUpperCase`) and prefixing it with `get` or `set` respectively.
The reason for this approach is that overriding a method and accessing the overridden method with a different context (`Function#call` or `Function#apply`) is easy.
The same does not hold true for proper ES5 getters and setters which have to be accessed via a property descriptor's `get` and `set` properties in order to invoke `Function#call` or `Function#apply` to pass a different context to those functions.

```javascript
b.accessor('foo');

var obj = b.create();

obj.foo;
// => obj.getFoo();

obj.foo = 42;
// => obj.setFoo(42);
```


### #assign(ext)

Assign own non-enumerable properties of a some object `ext` analogous to `Object.assign`.
This means it will evaluate getters instead of defining them like `#mixin`.

```javascript
b.assign({
  get foo() {
    return 123;
  }
});

var obj = b.create();
// => obj.foo === 123;
```


### #define(desc)

Define properties using `Object.defineProperties`.

```javascript
b.define({
  someConstant: {
    value: 'bar'
  }
});

var obj = b.create();
// => obj.someConstant === 'bar';
```


### #getter(...names)

Similar to `#accessor` but only defines getters.

```javascript
b.getter('foo');

var obj = b.create();

obj.foo;
// => obj.getFoo();
```


### #mixin(ext)

Mixin all own properties of a some object `ext` using `Object.defineProperty` and the individual property descriptors.

```javascript
b.mixin({
  someMethod: function () {
    console.log('yada yada yada');
  }
});

var obj = b.create();

obj.someMethod();
// => console.log('yada yada yada');
```


### #override(ext)

Override properties with own properties and the result of their respective functions in the given object.
Overridden properties are made accessible as first argument passed to the function returning the overriding value.
This argument is the overridden value when overriding an object's own property.
When overriding a property inherited via the prototype chain, the argument will be a function delegating to the current property accessible on the prototype.

```javascript
b.override({
  hello: function (_super) {
    return function () {
      return _super.call(this) + ' Doe';
    };
  }
});

var obj = {
  name: 'Jane',
  hello: function () {
    return 'Hello ' + this.name;
  }
};

b.apply(obj);

obj.hello();
// => 'Hello Jane Doe';
```


### #setter(...names)

Similar to `#accessor` but only defines setters.

```javascript
b.setter('foo');

var obj = b.create();

obj.foo = 42;
// => obj.setFoo(42);
```


